{:edb/db-before-t 2048 :edb/db-after-t 2049 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535361 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:45:59.712Z" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133396 1100 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 4x^3 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the line $z = 4-y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535361 false] [17592186133396 1100 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 4x^3  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the line $z = 4-y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535361 true] [17592186133396 1145 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4{x}^{3}\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{(\\sqrt{y})}^{4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/3c8712fa0a30ad94f503efe5e080ee89f83eb21f19fba45033bbedeab08ac816.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=4-y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{2}(4-y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}4{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(64-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(256-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133396 1145 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4x^{3}dx]dA &= ∬_(D)[x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = \\sqrt{y}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sqrt{y})^{4}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)y^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/f91b4d0b9ed33b8d327459a73fe31766.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)y^{2}dA &= ∫_{0}^{4}∫_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[∫_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dz]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[y^{2}z]_{z = 0}^{z = 4 - y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}y^{2}(4 - y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}4y^{2} - y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{4}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{1}{4}y^{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}(64 - 0) - \\frac{1}{4}(256 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 false] [17592186133397 1100 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133397 1100 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = \\left\\{(y,z) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}$." 13194139535361 false] [17592186133397 1145 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{z}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dx]dA &= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[x]_{x = 0}^{x = z}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)(z - 0)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA &= ∫_{0}^{π/6}∫_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1 - y}}(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[\\frac{z^{2}}{2}]_{z = 0}^{z = 2\\sqrt{1 - y}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)((4(1 - y))/(2) - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/6}2\\cos (2y)dy \\\\\n&= [\\sin (2y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/6} \\\\\n&= \\sin (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\sin (0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 false] [17592186133397 1145 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}[x]_{x=0}^{x=z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}(z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-y}}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2\\sqrt{1-y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}(\\frac{4(1-y)}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133403 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/fc19dd4415663ab027e0fc386d4c5bc81a28c1bc26d5f6cf2083f4f4228a4dea.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133403 1100 "Evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/e1e0a37a01b5ecf240851daf1ff21f56.png)\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535361 false] [17592186133403 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/f25c393f10c35ffd9b4fa91cdf672a516cf6cff974a8aa563d350619a64b79bd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/145f0d494ffe24de84e3a289b3a7316d3f4933f193d734aed118609f7fb9f94f.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint \\limits_{R}  12z^2  \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{3-y-z}  12z^2  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-y-z}12{z}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-y-z}12{z}^{2}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{z}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=3-y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{z}^{2}(3-y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{z}^{2}-12y{z}^{2}-12{z}^{3})\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{z}^{2}-12y{z}^{2}-12{z}^{3})\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{z}^{2}-12y{z}^{2}-12{z}^{3})\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[12{z}^{3}-4y{z}^{3}-3{z}^{4}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12-4y-3)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-4y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-4y)\\,dy & =[9y-2{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(9-2)-0 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133403 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/a4f8d3e95c46f652b90983ba8bfa9d97.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/5b209d6360c4381ea8e24a7a857c8cba.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint \\limits_{R} 12z^2 \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{3-y-z} 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{3 - y - z}12z^{2}dxdzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{3 - y - z}12z^{2}dx]dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}12z^{2}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = 3 - y - z}dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}12z^{2}(3 - y - z)dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dzdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dz]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[12z^{3} - 4yz^{3} - 3z^{4}]_{z = 0}^{z = 1}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(12 - 4y - 3)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(9 - 4y)dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(9 - 4y)dy &= [9y - 2y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= (9 - 2) - 0 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 false] [17592186133405 1100 "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \nf(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region defined as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y}, \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq ye^{z+2} \\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133405 1100 "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region defined as\n\n$R=\\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y}, \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq ye^{z+2} \\right\\}$." 13194139535361 false] [17592186133405 1145 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq u_2(y,z) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm{d}x \\right]\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(y,z) = -2, \\qquad u_2(y,z)  = ye^{z+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{-2}^{ye^{z+2}} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2}^{y{e}^{z+2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}[{\\int }_{-2}^{y{e}^{z+2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{-2}^{y{e}^{z+2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186133405 1145 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq u_2(y,z) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/83d78c3a80d9921c38438e2967052d69.png)\n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm{d}x \\right]\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(y,z) = -2, \\qquad u_2(y,z) = ye^{z+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{-2}^{ye^{z+2}} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[∫_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dx]dA &= ∫_{1}^{4}∫_{-y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}[∫_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dx]dzdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{4}∫_{-y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}∫_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 false] [17592186133837 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345834 13194139535361 true] [17592186133837 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345841 13194139535361 true] [17592186133837 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345848 13194139535361 true] [17592186133837 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345855 13194139535361 true] [17592186133838 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345785 13194139535361 true] [17592186133838 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345792 13194139535361 true] [17592186133838 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345820 13194139535361 true] [17592186133838 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345827 13194139535361 true] [17592186133845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345862 13194139535361 true] [17592186133845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345869 13194139535361 true] [17592186133845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345876 13194139535361 true] [17592186133845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345883 13194139535361 true] [17592186133847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345771 13194139535361 true] [17592186133847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345778 13194139535361 true] [17592186133847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345799 13194139535361 true] [17592186133847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345806 13194139535361 true] [17592186133847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186345813 13194139535361 true] [17592186134193 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535361 true] [17592186134193 1100 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$∭_(R)2ydV$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (y, z) ∈ D, 0 \\le x \\le z\\sqrt{3}}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y = 2$ and the line $z = y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535361 false] [17592186134193 1100 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le z\\sqrt{3}},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y=2$ and the line $z=y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535361 true] [17592186134193 1145 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z\\sqrt{3}}2y\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z\\sqrt{3}}2y\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}2y[x]_{x=0}^{x=z\\sqrt{3}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/63a8768b2769fad339ba7667357812d2dda0ea856e846ad666a100f8bc1336e9.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2z\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{16}{4}-0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186134196 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535361 true] [17592186134196 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/bc1b0ea463b0f753a2c65784fa5d46203cde8f457116c481301bf3f20ce82f54.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4y\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535361 true] [17592186134196 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2141/Source/Images/q-40219.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)4ydV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535361 false] [17592186134196 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/9bb9311cbaf796f76c4b8b7cb7ed81abec48abc445b894f838f940fbd92e534b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063435/assets/82f237dc5fb7d8b8ed34b7a1c16527ab338923a33462f0bd7dafbe54bc5e5158.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4y\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4y[x]_{x=0}^{x=4-y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4y(4-y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[16yz-4{y}^{2}z-2y{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(14y-4{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(14y-4{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={[7{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(28-\\frac{32}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186134210 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535361 true] [17592186134210 1100 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535361 true] [17592186134210 1100 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{y}12zdx]dA$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = {(y, z): 0 \\le y \\le 2, 0 \\le z \\le 4 - y^{2}}$." 13194139535361 false] [17592186134210 1145 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬